1
00:00:01,562 --> 00:00:04,391
TV NEWS: At sunrise, the No-Force Law begins.

2
00:00:04,410 --> 00:00:07,866
TV NEWS: No one, not police, not soldiers, not anyone,

3
00:00:07,886 --> 00:00:10,695
TV NEWS: may touch another person without consent.

4
00:00:10,715 --> 00:00:14,957
TV NEWS: And no one, for any reason, may be locked in a cage.

5
00:00:14,977 --> 00:00:18,771
NARRATOR: Imagine a world where force is off the table.

6
00:00:18,791 --> 00:00:20,774
NARRATOR: For everyone.

7
00:00:20,794 --> 00:00:22,524
NARRATOR: And if someone breaks that rule,

8
00:00:22,544 --> 00:00:25,413
NARRATOR: the answer still can't be force.

9
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:29,213
NARRATOR: Only words, distance, and the people around you.

10
00:00:30,175 --> 00:00:31,512
KAI: Refill?

11
00:00:31,532 --> 00:00:32,912
HALE: Please.

12
00:00:33,933 --> 00:00:35,886
KAI: Last night with those?

13
00:00:35,906 --> 00:00:38,086
HALE: Last night with all of it.

14
00:00:38,763 --> 00:00:40,156
KAI: It's dead tonight.

15
00:00:40,176 --> 00:00:41,583
KAI: Mind if I sit?

16
00:00:41,603 --> 00:00:42,930
HALE: It's your diner.

17
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:44,130
KAI: It's your booth.

18
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:45,580
KAI: So I asked.

19
00:00:46,407 --> 00:00:48,836
HALE: Twenty-two years I've carried these.

20
00:00:48,856 --> 00:00:52,202
HALE: Tomorrow I'm supposed to keep people safe with what?

21
00:00:52,222 --> 00:00:53,402
HALE: Words?

22
00:00:53,422 --> 00:00:54,317
KAI: Words.

23
00:00:54,337 --> 00:00:55,360
KAI: Witnesses.

24
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:58,022
KAI: Your body, if you choose to stand somewhere.

25
00:00:58,042 --> 00:01:00,909
HALE: And when someone swings a bottle at somebody's head?

26
00:01:00,929 --> 00:01:02,991
KAI: You reach for the person getting hurt.

27
00:01:03,011 --> 00:01:05,007
KAI: If they take your hand, you get them out.

28
00:01:05,027 --> 00:01:06,313
KAI: You stand in the way.

29
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:08,670
KAI: You get the whole room yelling stop.

30
00:01:08,690 --> 00:01:11,869
NARRATOR: You can't grab the person causing harm.

31
00:01:11,889 --> 00:01:14,119
NARRATOR: But you can reach for the person being harmed,

32
00:01:14,139 --> 00:01:16,229
NARRATOR: if they reach back.

33
00:01:16,249 --> 00:01:19,804
NARRATOR: And a lot of violence depends on not being seen.

34
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:22,422
HALE: And when that doesn't work?

35
00:01:22,962 --> 00:01:24,619
KAI: Then sometimes,

36
00:01:24,639 --> 00:01:26,699
KAI: someone gets hurt.

37
00:01:26,719 --> 00:01:29,111
NARRATOR: That's the honest cost.

38
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:30,398
NARRATOR: Some harm that force could

39
00:01:30,418 --> 00:01:33,351
NARRATOR: have stopped will happen anyway.

40
00:01:33,371 --> 00:01:36,723
NARRATOR: But force has its own victims, too.

41
00:01:36,743 --> 00:01:40,023
NARRATOR: This world bets that the trade is worth it.

42
00:01:41,261 --> 00:01:44,912
NARRATOR: So at sunrise, every cell door opens.

43
00:01:44,932 --> 00:01:48,130
NARRATOR: More than eleven million people walk out.

44
00:01:48,150 --> 00:01:50,694
NARRATOR: Many were never convicted.

45
00:01:50,714 --> 00:01:54,166
NARRATOR: Many are sick, or addicted, or poor.

46
00:01:54,186 --> 00:01:57,016
NARRATOR: And some did terrible things.

47
00:01:57,769 --> 00:01:59,301
HALE: That's what scares me.

48
00:01:59,321 --> 00:02:01,592
HALE: The ones who really hurt people.

49
00:02:01,612 --> 00:02:02,568
KAI: Yeah.

50
00:02:02,588 --> 00:02:04,003
KAI: Me too.

51
00:02:04,023 --> 00:02:06,953
NARRATOR: They still exist in this world.

52
00:02:06,973 --> 00:02:08,903
NARRATOR: The rule only changes what everyone

53
00:02:08,923 --> 00:02:11,778
NARRATOR: else is allowed to do about them.

54
00:02:12,555 --> 00:02:14,310
NARRATOR: If someone keeps hurting people,

55
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:17,003
NARRATOR: the community can step back.

56
00:02:17,023 --> 00:02:19,621
NARRATOR: Distance isn't a cage.

57
00:02:19,641 --> 00:02:21,534
NARRATOR: It's a boundary.

58
00:02:21,554 --> 00:02:24,383
NARRATOR: And the door stays open.

59
00:02:24,403 --> 00:02:26,452
KAI: And when they're ready, there's a circle.

60
00:02:26,472 --> 00:02:28,342
KAI: Everyone they hurt gets to talk.

61
00:02:28,362 --> 00:02:30,139
KAI: And they have to listen.

62
00:02:30,159 --> 00:02:32,526
NARRATOR: Where circles like this are already used,

63
00:02:32,546 --> 00:02:34,989
NARRATOR: victims report feeling more satisfied,

64
00:02:35,009 --> 00:02:38,289
NARRATOR: and less hungry for revenge, than after court.

65
00:02:39,178 --> 00:02:41,017
HALE: So what's my job now?

66
00:02:41,037 --> 00:02:42,233
KAI: Same as always.

67
00:02:42,253 --> 00:02:43,652
KAI: Keep people safe.

68
00:02:43,672 --> 00:02:45,593
KAI: You just lost the shortcut.

69
00:02:45,613 --> 00:02:47,436
HALE: The shortcut.

70
00:02:47,456 --> 00:02:51,088
NARRATOR: Most police calls aren't violent anyway.

71
00:02:51,108 --> 00:02:52,269
NARRATOR: Noise.

72
00:02:52,289 --> 00:02:53,902
NARRATOR: Car crashes.

73
00:02:53,922 --> 00:02:56,907
NARRATOR: Someone in crisis on a bridge.

74
00:02:56,927 --> 00:02:59,407
NARRATOR: Here, those calls go to people trained to talk,

75
00:02:59,427 --> 00:03:02,053
NARRATOR: to treat, and to stay.

76
00:03:02,073 --> 00:03:04,090
NARRATOR: And the money once spent on cages goes

77
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:07,203
NARRATOR: into what stops harm before it starts.

78
00:03:07,929 --> 00:03:09,156
HALE: And soldiers?

79
00:03:09,176 --> 00:03:11,575
HALE: What if someone rolls in with tanks?

80
00:03:11,595 --> 00:03:15,218
NARRATOR: Then the only defense left is refusal.

81
00:03:15,238 --> 00:03:16,372
NARRATOR: Strikes.

82
00:03:16,392 --> 00:03:17,996
NARRATOR: Empty streets.

83
00:03:18,016 --> 00:03:20,039
NARRATOR: No one obeying.

84
00:03:20,059 --> 00:03:22,326
NARRATOR: In a study of a century of uprisings,

85
00:03:22,346 --> 00:03:24,376
NARRATOR: nonviolent campaigns succeeded

86
00:03:24,396 --> 00:03:27,689
NARRATOR: about twice as often as violent ones.

87
00:03:29,586 --> 00:03:30,967
KAI: You don't remember me,

88
00:03:30,987 --> 00:03:32,389
KAI: do you?

89
00:03:32,409 --> 00:03:34,072
HALE: Should I know you?

90
00:03:34,092 --> 00:03:35,325
KAI: Fourth Street.

91
00:03:35,345 --> 00:03:36,690
KAI: Three years ago.

92
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:38,337
KAI: I was fifteen.

93
00:03:38,357 --> 00:03:40,812
KAI: You told me I matched a description.

94
00:03:41,448 --> 00:03:42,781
KAI: I didn't.

95
00:03:42,801 --> 00:03:45,692
KAI: You held my face against the hood of your car.

96
00:03:45,712 --> 00:03:47,761
KAI: The metal was so cold.

97
00:03:48,866 --> 00:03:50,546
HALE: I remember.

98
00:03:51,258 --> 00:03:54,559
NARRATOR: In the old world, this is where it ends.

99
00:03:54,579 --> 00:03:56,573
NARRATOR: She drives home.

100
00:03:56,593 --> 00:03:58,481
NARRATOR: He carries it.

101
00:03:58,501 --> 00:04:01,499
NARRATOR: In this world, he can call it out.

102
00:04:01,519 --> 00:04:03,349
NARRATOR: And she can listen.

103
00:04:04,157 --> 00:04:06,633
KAI: I still flinch when a car door slams.

104
00:04:06,653 --> 00:04:08,806
KAI: I've drawn that night a hundred times,

105
00:04:08,826 --> 00:04:10,856
KAI: trying to make it smaller.

106
00:04:11,676 --> 00:04:13,481
HALE: I'm sorry, Kai.

107
00:04:13,501 --> 00:04:14,750
HALE: I was scared,

108
00:04:14,770 --> 00:04:16,925
HALE: and I made you pay for it.

109
00:04:18,003 --> 00:04:20,366
KAI: I don't know if I forgive you.

110
00:04:20,386 --> 00:04:21,916
HALE: You don't have to.

111
00:04:22,768 --> 00:04:25,001
KAI: Then what happens now?

112
00:04:25,021 --> 00:04:26,393
HALE: Now I listen.

113
00:04:26,413 --> 00:04:28,543
HALE: As long as you want to talk.

114
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:31,214
NARRATOR: No cage.

115
00:04:31,234 --> 00:04:32,911
NARRATOR: No fist.

116
00:04:32,931 --> 00:04:34,561
NARRATOR: Just the truth, out loud,

117
00:04:34,581 --> 00:04:37,186
NARRATOR: and someone who stays to hear it.

118
00:04:39,363 --> 00:04:41,143
HALE: Can I shake your hand?

119
00:04:41,944 --> 00:04:43,238
KAI: Fist bump.

120
00:04:43,258 --> 00:04:44,963
HALE: Fist bump it is.

121
00:04:45,531 --> 00:04:49,824
NARRATOR: In a world without force, every touch is a question.

122
00:04:49,844 --> 00:04:52,674
NARRATOR: Sometimes, the answer is yes.

123
00:04:53,797 --> 00:04:55,576
NARRATOR: Would it work?

124
00:04:55,596 --> 00:04:59,399
NARRATOR: No society this big has ever tried.

125
00:04:59,419 --> 00:05:01,049
NARRATOR: But if no one can be forced,

126
00:05:01,069 --> 00:05:05,010
NARRATOR: the only power left is the kind you have to earn.

127
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:07,509
NARRATOR: The power to protect.

128
00:05:07,529 --> 00:05:09,237
NARRATOR: To persuade.

129
00:05:09,257 --> 00:05:10,887
NARRATOR: And to care.
